            <ArticleTitle>5-Hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) and Ten-eleven translocation 1-3 (TET1-3) proteins in the dorsal root ganglia of mouse: Expression and dynamic regulation in neuropathic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>72-79</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/08990220.2017.1292237</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Epigenetic mechanisms are increasingly implicated in chronic pain pathology. In this study, we demonstrate that the novel epigenetic mark 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) is present in dorsal root ganglia (DRG) neurons and glia, and its levels increase following nerve injury. Furthermore, we show that the 5hmC-generating Ten-eleven translocation 1-3 (TET1-3) proteins are expressed in a cell-type specific manner in the DRG, with Tet3 displaying differential upregulation after injury, suggesting a potential role in neuropathic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
